.TH UD80 1 "November 2025" "um80 0.3.3" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ud80 \- 8080/Z80 disassembler for CP/M .COM files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ud80
[\fB\-v\fR]
[\fB\-z\fR]
[\fB\-o\fR \fIoutput.mac\fR]
[\fB\-e\fR \fIaddress\fR[\fB,\fR\fIlabel\fR]]...
[\fB\-l\fR \fIaddress\fR[\fB,\fR\fIlabel\fR]]...
[\fB\-d\fR \fIstart\-end\fR]...
[\fB\-t\fR \fIstart\-end\fR]...
[\fB\-dc\fR \fIstart\-end\fR]...
[\fB\-da\fR \fIstart\-end\fR[\fB,\fR\fIlength\fR]]...
[\fB\-\-org\fR \fIaddress\fR]
.I input.com
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ud80
is a disassembler for 8080 and Z80 binary files.
It writes output that is compatible with the
.BR um80 (1)
assembler.
It converts CP/M .COM executable files (or raw binary files) back
into assembly source code.
You can modify the source code and assemble it again.
.PP
ud80 does a flow analysis from the entry points.
The flow analysis distinguishes code from data.
ud80 writes unvisited bytes as DB directives.
The design goal is that um80 assembles the output to an identical binary.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-v\fR, \fB\-\-version
Print version information and exit.
.TP
.BR \-z ", " \-\-z80
Enable Z80 mode. In this mode:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
ud80 decodes the Z80 extended instructions (prefixes CB, DD, ED and FD)
.IP \(bu 2
ud80 recognizes the undocumented Z80 instructions
.IP \(bu 2
ud80 uses Zilog mnemonics in the output
.RE
.IP
Without this option, ud80 decodes only 8080 instructions.
ud80 then uses Intel mnemonics.
.TP
.BI \-o " output.mac"
Set the name of the output file.
The default is the name of the input file with a .mac extension.
.TP
.BI \-e " address" "\fR[\fB,\fIlabel\fR]"
Add an entry point at the specified hexadecimal address.
Flow analysis traces the execution from this address.
You can repeat this option.
.IP
Optionally, write a label name after a comma, for example
\fB\-e 297a,ends\fR.
ud80 uses this label in the output in place of a generated label.
.IP
By default, ud80 starts at the origin address.
For CP/M programs, the origin address is typically 0100h.
To add entry points for code that normal flow does not reach, use \-e.
Examples of such code are interrupt handlers and jump tables.
.TP
.BI \-l " address" "\fR[\fB,\fIlabel\fR]"
Add a label at the specified address.
ud80 does not trace the address as code.
You can repeat this option.
.IP
Optionally, write a label name after a comma, for example
\fB\-l 4406,buffer\fR.
If you give no label name, ud80 uses a generated label (L\fIxxxx\fR).
.IP
Use this option for data labels.
Use this option also for code that you want to label but not trace,
for example when you know that other means already reach the code.
.TP
.BI \-d " start\-end"
Force a range of addresses to be data and not code.
\fIstart\fR and \fIend\fR are hexadecimal addresses.
The range is inclusive.
You can repeat this option.
.IP
Use this option for embedded data tables.
Otherwise, ud80 can disassemble such tables incorrectly as instructions.
.TP
.BI \-t " start\-end"
Mark a range of addresses as an address table.
ud80 writes the range as DW (define word) directives with labels where
possible, not as individual DB bytes.
\fIstart\fR and \fIend\fR are hexadecimal addresses.
You can repeat this option.
.IP
Use this option for jump tables or dispatch tables that contain 16-bit
addresses.
ud80 writes the table entries as follows:
.RS
.nf
DW      L1234
DW      L5678
\&...
.fi
.RE
.TP
.BI \-dc " start\-end"
Mark a range as high-bit terminated strings.
In these strings, the last character has bit 7 set (OR 80H).
Keyword tables often have such strings.
\fIstart\fR and \fIend\fR are hexadecimal addresses.
You can repeat this option.
.IP
The output uses the DC directive.
The DC directive sets the high bit on the last character:
.RS
.nf
DC      'ABC'
.fi
.RE
.IP
Each \-dc option marks one string region.
Do not include token bytes or other data between strings in the range.
.TP
.BI \-da " start\-end" "\fR[,\fIlength\fR]"
Mark a range as ASCII strings.
If you give no length parameter, strings end at a null byte (00H) or at any
non-printable character.
If you give a length, the strings have a fixed length.
\fIstart\fR and \fIend\fR are hexadecimal addresses.
You can repeat this option.
.IP
Examples:
.RS
.nf
\-da 0200\-02FF          # Strings end at null or non-printable
\-da 0200\-02FF,8        # Fixed 8-character strings
.fi
.RE
.IP
Output format: \fBDB 'HELLO'\fR
.br
ud80 writes any null terminator as a separate \fBDB 00H\fR directive.
.TP
.BI \-\-org " address"
Set the origin address (where the binary is loaded in memory).
The default is 0100h, the standard CP/M transient program area.
The address is in hexadecimal.
.SH FLOW ANALYSIS
.B ud80
does a recursive descent disassembly from the entry points.
The steps are:
.IP 1. 3
Begin with each entry point.
.IP 2.
Decode the instructions and follow the sequential execution.
.IP 3.
When ud80 encounters a branch (for example JMP, JNZ or CALL), queue the
branch target.
.IP 4.
Stop the trace at an unconditional jump, a return or a halt.
.IP 5.
Continue until the trace covers all reachable code.
.PP
.B Post-jump gap detection:
After an unconditional jump (JMP, RET, HLT), the bytes that immediately follow
are typically data and not code.
ud80 automatically detects these "post-jump gaps" and marks them as data.
The marking prevents false code traces.
Such traces might otherwise occur if stale addresses happen to point into
these regions.
.PP
.B Automatic jump table detection:
ud80 automatically detects address tables (jump tables and dispatch tables)
with two methods:
.IP \(bu 2
.B Post-entry tables:
Consider the bytes after the initial JMP at an entry point.
If these bytes form a sequence of valid addresses, ud80 treats the bytes as an
address table.
A sequence has 3 or more consecutive 16-bit values that point within the code
range.
.IP \(bu 2
.B Dispatch pattern detection:
A code sequence like "LXI H,addr / DAD B" indicates a table lookup.
ud80 recognizes the pattern and marks the referenced address as a table base.
.PP
ud80 writes automatically detected tables as DW directives with labels.
The labels make the disassembly more readable.
.PP
This approach correctly distinguishes code from data in most cases.
The approach can miss code that only a computed jump or an interrupt vector
reaches.
.SH OUTPUT FORMAT
The output is a valid
.BR um80 (1)
source file:
.PP
.RS
.nf
; Disassembly of program.com
; Generated by ud80

        ORG     0100H

L0100:  JMP     L0150       ; Jump to main
L0103:  DB      0,0,0       ; Data bytes
        ...
L0150:  MVI     A,0         ; Main entry
        ...
        END
.fi
.RE
.PP
Features of the output:
.IP \(bu 2
ud80 writes labels for all branch targets (L\fIxxxx\fR format)
.IP \(bu 2
ud80 writes unreachable bytes as DB directives
.IP \(bu 2
ud80 shows string-like data as DB with ASCII strings where possible
.PP
.B Note:
ud80 writes the END directive without a start address label.
In CP/M, execution always begins at the load address (typically 0100H).
Thus the start address is implicit.
The first linked module determines where execution starts.
An END with an explicit label would only be useful for non-CP/M targets
(such as ROM-based systems).
On these targets, a startup JMP would be inserted at the load address.
.SH 8080 vs Z80 MODE
.SS 8080 Mode (default)
ud80 uses Intel 8080 mnemonics:
.IP \(bu 2
MOV, MVI, LXI for data movement
.IP \(bu 2
ADD, ADC, SUB, SBB for arithmetic
.IP \(bu 2
ANA, XRA, ORA for logic
.IP \(bu 2
JMP, JNZ, JZ, CALL, RET for control flow
.IP \(bu 2
Registers: A, B, C, D, E, H, L, M, SP, PSW
.SS Z80 Mode (\-z)
ud80 uses Zilog Z80 mnemonics:
.IP \(bu 2
LD for most data movement
.IP \(bu 2
ADD, ADC, SUB, SBC for arithmetic
.IP \(bu 2
AND, XOR, OR for logic
.IP \(bu 2
JP, JR, CALL, RET, DJNZ for control flow
.IP \(bu 2
Additional registers: IX, IY, I, R
.IP \(bu 2
Extended instructions: for example LDIR, CPIR and bit operations
.PP
Z80 mode also decodes the instructions with the prefixes CB, DD, ED and FD.
The decoded instructions include undocumented opcodes for the registers IXH,
IXL, IYH and IYL.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Basic disassembly:
.B ud80 program.com
.TP
Z80 mode:
.B ud80 \-z program.com
.TP
Custom output file:
.B ud80 \-o source.mac program.com
.TP
Add entry point:
.B ud80 \-e 0200 program.com
.TP
Multiple entry points:
.B ud80 \-e 0200 \-e 0300 \-e 0400 program.com
.TP
Force data range:
.B ud80 \-d 0500\-05FF program.com
.TP
Mark jump table (output as DW):
.B ud80 \-t 0103\-0120 program.com
.TP
Non-CP/M binary at 0000h:
.B ud80 \-\-org 0000 bios.bin
.TP
Combine options:
.B ud80 \-z \-e 0200 \-d 0300\-03FF \-t 0103\-0120 \-o source.mac game.com
.SH ROUND-TRIP VERIFICATION
To verify the quality of the disassembly, assemble the output again and compare
the files:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ud80 program.com
um80 \-\-aseg program.mac
ul80 \-o program.new program.rel
cmp program.com program.new
.fi
.RE
.PP
If the files match, the disassembly is accurate.
.SH TIPS
.SS Finding Entry Points
.IP \(bu 2
Find the interrupt vectors at fixed addresses, for example RST handlers
.IP \(bu 2
Find the jump table patterns
.IP \(bu 2
Examine call targets that flow analysis missed
.SS Identifying Data
.IP \(bu 2
Strings of ASCII characters
.IP \(bu 2
Tables of addresses (pairs of bytes)
.IP \(bu 2
Sequences that do not decode as valid instructions
.IP \(bu 2
Areas after unconditional jumps that no branch targets
.SS Iterative Refinement
.IP 1. 3
Run the first disassembly.
.IP 2.
Examine the output for data that ud80 decoded incorrectly.
.IP 3.
Add \-d options for data regions.
.IP 4.
Add \-e options for missed code.
.IP 5.
Disassemble again and verify the result.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Disassembly successful.
.TP
.B 1
An error occurred, for example file not found or invalid address.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I input.com
Input binary file (CP/M executable or raw binary).
.TP
.I output.mac
Output assembly source file.
.SH LIMITATIONS
.IP \(bu 2
ud80 cannot disassemble self-modifying code correctly.
.IP \(bu 2
Computed jumps, for example JP (HL), do not contribute to flow analysis.
.IP \(bu 2
ud80 cannot process overlay areas or banked memory automatically.
.IP \(bu 2
ud80 does not annotate CP/M system calls.
An annotation would require additional analysis.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR um80 (1),
.BR ul80 (1),
.BR ulib80 (1),
.BR ucref80 (1)
.SH HISTORY
.B ud80
was created as a companion tool for the um80 assembler toolchain.
Unlike ud80, the other tools are implementations of Microsoft originals.
ud80 is a new tool that the designers made to complement the toolchain.
ud80 disassembles existing CP/M programs.
.SH AUTHOR
um80 project contributors.
